@yohairosen for your use case, what you need to do is define 2 different roles. What's wrong with this implementation:
ac.grant('salesTeamManager')
  .readOwn('teamSales')
  .grant('salesManager')
  .readAny('teamSales');

Both roles are granted permissions for the teamSales resource.
We'll now fetch the resource representation/instance by a team identifier.
REST Endpoint:
GET /team/:teamId/sales

SQL (if fetching from a database):
SELECT * FROM sales WHERE teamId = :teamId;

Node/Express App Handler:
app.get('/team/:teamId/sales', (req, res) => {
  const user = req.user;
  const targetTeamId = req.params.teamId;
  const userTeamInfo = service.getUserTeamInfo(user.id);

  const can = ac.can(user.role);
  // permission by possession
  const permission = (userTeamInfo.id === targetTeamId)
    ? can.readOwn('teamSales')
    : can.readAny('teamSales');

  if (permission.granted) {
    const data = service.getTeamSales(targetTeamId);
    res.json(permission.filter(data));
  } else {
    res.status(403).end();
  }
});

Bottom line; it's not -only- how you control access, it's important how you design the application in the first place.
Hope this answers your question.


